#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8958422f-4c02-53af-a3e5-28f8445d5bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377710} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7dc054-8a42-5213-b120-534bafd7ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377711} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad9cc3c-2948-54af-96ab-97ac3dc5dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377712} {:answer/id #uuid "de9768d4-de86-55ac-a72b-b0470b48216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2466e9-2081-57a2-b9cc-0260610356d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9768d4-de86-55ac-a72b-b0470b48216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14349ec-0b87-5b7c-b87c-2368f3340f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6323a167-9fc1-5848-81f1-63d34a9c63ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-74965" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da8b2db2-4123-5665-b738-98ed34b75b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378624} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3687c4-99c4-5acf-aa17-8ee4a667504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378625} {:answer/id #uuid "5cfdc49f-f710-579f-a121-34bd82cc68c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378626} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef6f1c-9c50-5077-83d6-7937a6ae0e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378627} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4dca40-4471-5b61-bf8b-e5086c017493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4dca40-4471-5b61-bf8b-e5086c017493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89502e5-3b74-56d2-882e-fcb6a0ee1e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88dbd745-2040-5337-99bf-90ad013cc0a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129327" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54bad6f7-afaf-5211-a170-c6ab451f5c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378631} {:answer/id #uuid "b3eb5e8a-c30b-5e5d-b3dd-5e84a503fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378632} {:answer/id #uuid "9a772183-f18a-5957-a18c-b8eaf33e3416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378633} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d48428-fe8b-54bd-8382-9007264d0a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378634} {:answer/id #uuid "816585ea-530d-530b-acda-63e8b7815463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3eb5e8a-c30b-5e5d-b3dd-5e84a503fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378632} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2035409-00f4-5463-8bd1-cb9d0396a3d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bb79452-99aa-5770-9532-a1808e3b1fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129334" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed9a8298-54cd-5904-87cc-0773ceed9484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378638} {:answer/id #uuid "16292b9f-eddf-569d-8d1e-2dd760b68415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378639} {:answer/id #uuid "40076bfe-6573-5b16-91f1-cdb51a556125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378640} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea9746-2601-59cf-97ee-1ee69879d0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378641} {:answer/id #uuid "6cca0148-f328-5676-8042-40e9ef488295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40076bfe-6573-5b16-91f1-cdb51a556125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f471c6a4-8548-5953-bc64-722a32227684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a45c5a39-df0e-5ca7-830f-279fde3ab785" :question/math-academy-id "q-129403" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a685e0-c5f1-5af2-896d-00b1934e31d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380043} {:answer/id #uuid "24ca23db-bb35-57cc-a828-65a684d47d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380044} {:answer/id #uuid "395c8b3a-034c-5c76-8114-a06f611b180e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380045} {:answer/id #uuid "19c8cd1c-6dc3-50a3-9875-7799ef849dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380046} {:answer/id #uuid "8088eaef-6ff9-5ae3-a905-dce7305a0cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395c8b3a-034c-5c76-8114-a06f611b180e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380045} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b999621-685d-57ae-875e-b93bc57b0422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6df7cb51-78c3-5b67-a139-cdde39ca3596" :question/math-academy-id "q-74910" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d0157d0-7447-5804-b79a-d9a9496f35c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380050} {:answer/id #uuid "44b22efb-654b-53b0-b5bd-3dbebc607aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380051} {:answer/id #uuid "0767a721-3b28-5414-a3e4-a98c298c7320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380052} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf8c18e-328f-5ffc-8a5c-aec2375f7d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380053} {:answer/id #uuid "d27bd26c-9c31-59e2-8622-050a5d783466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf8c18e-328f-5ffc-8a5c-aec2375f7d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380053} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd8a32c-5a54-5319-9722-fa439ed2e8e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70818c9b-be75-5377-b1b9-218a9a896c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74913" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "392ee925-0a86-58e9-be9d-088d11882899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380057} {:answer/id #uuid "95b9f301-3e4c-5b4a-8f90-c92aeb09e382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380058} {:answer/id #uuid "988389d2-83d7-50e4-b103-318efb7628de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380059} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0a15c7-e641-5ba1-805d-360eea7a6554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380060} {:answer/id #uuid "b8655f26-6734-5a2e-bdb9-ca9cf96b1f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f0a15c7-e641-5ba1-805d-360eea7a6554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380060} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e2ee50-e13c-591e-b10c-41485a4529eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "142437af-f631-571c-967f-34ef02138536" :question/math-academy-id "q-74919" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87ce1ae9-57eb-59cd-8da4-c1a0d3076afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453952} {:answer/id #uuid "ebff1072-dded-550a-b750-8d400dcf93f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87ce1ae9-57eb-59cd-8da4-c1a0d3076afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453952} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e09413b-af06-58f6-88c6-125426ab31d4" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454726} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776c0c2e-2277-507d-b18d-ed1e84b4ed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453954} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3dfc88-d38f-581f-9c20-80e64cbb04cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b3dfc88-d38f-581f-9c20-80e64cbb04cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453955} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ee9fb1-8544-5a36-b32c-707df9287960" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ca50ed-9faf-5c4a-bf22-4d644e34ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453956} {:answer/id #uuid "161c0d73-cc78-5569-9199-8fc986254f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ca50ed-9faf-5c4a-bf22-4d644e34ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453956} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b63f72f-ca55-514b-bc37-6bef9f319ce0" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb1f3bd1-4155-5e86-902f-cd91483f7d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-74963" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a6788-72f7-50c1-a035-0612abea7cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380244} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6b773-681c-58f9-b0ee-b6ee7442117a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380245} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a1ac-d347-5b5f-9ae4-3572ba829da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380246} {:answer/id #uuid "91b1447d-4781-51f6-b677-110d8689bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} {:answer/id #uuid "8852c79a-f54e-5a8f-87c0-2803442d73d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91b1447d-4781-51f6-b677-110d8689bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f7b683-d099-548b-9b98-dddfeba7f07a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb25e06c-c7b0-5dbe-83ae-8f7b0c5dbf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-86868" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b106ba1-7d67-5f7a-a845-e1b9a09768d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,949$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383496} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf1499c-e3da-5fac-ae71-771b639b5ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,452$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383497} {:answer/id #uuid "d8005cdc-6015-5b68-933d-10f9a60c85fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,352$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383498} {:answer/id #uuid "6a511af0-d99a-55b6-9701-9bd6bd0e1ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,243$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383499} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c46db4-942a-5ee1-9e5a-085e796f30e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5\\,432$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b106ba1-7d67-5f7a-a845-e1b9a09768d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,949$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383496} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad4c947-eac2-545f-9549-99ba72d2a451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60292909-ce24-593a-b903-368e85e24430" :question/math-academy-id "q-15229" :question/problem "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $70\\%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5\\,730$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,730=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.7{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.7{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.7\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.7 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.7) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5\\,730\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.7)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2\\,949\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $2\\,949$ years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635d2341-3a33-5ac9-bc90-9aa868281876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385210} {:answer/id #uuid "923fae5f-f36e-59d7-a2f9-4b1a32154401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385211} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc28125-aa6b-5b7d-9978-9da8bc0bd171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385213} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd0d588-4274-52c3-9c9a-c8c45c106ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} :answer-field/id #uuid "711b7022-c824-5eae-90a8-e7d790b19008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c6933cf-ef37-5bf5-8816-b51b8e56e9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336501" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+15t-10t=5(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =9(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =9(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(20+t)-\\frac{40(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(20+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 100+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 100+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =16\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(16) & =3(20+16)-40{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+16}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =108-40{\\left(\\frac{20}{36}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈95.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fff374-e865-54f3-a150-c5efb630780a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} {:answer/id #uuid "7fddeb67-7d2e-5a6b-8751-43edc5489a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385218} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6744f-c23c-5448-817b-4573055d4ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "129.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385219} {:answer/id #uuid "e29bac7e-c5b7-5397-996e-06f067a62576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385220} {:answer/id #uuid "01805774-f2d7-5739-bed3-d2bf97e1dfc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fff374-e865-54f3-a150-c5efb630780a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7d8e34-4951-5d39-86d9-5779f3f14045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d3bb996-2c5a-57e9-940a-6b48f2e05670" :question/math-academy-id "q-336502" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $160\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+6t-4t=2(50+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(50+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|50+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(50+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(50+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q) & =(50+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (50+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(50+t)Q & =6(50+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2}) & =6(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(50+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(50+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(50+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(50+t)+\\frac{C}{(50+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(50+0)+\\frac{C}{(50+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 100+\\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =-75 \\\\ C & =-75(50)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(50+t)-\\frac{75(50)^{2}}{(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(50+t)-75{\\left(\\frac{50}{50+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $160\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=160$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =160 \\\\ 100+6{t}_{o}-4{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 100+2{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =2(50+30)-75{\\left(\\frac{50}{50+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =160-75{\\left(\\frac{50}{80}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈130.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3eb411b-62d3-53c6-80e4-868eb7539f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c74a4cf84ce908132b57674ef5624e0afcd42af7c40150ac0079e25894a60ba3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386961} {:answer/id #uuid "e90af615-1ee8-5498-898e-f0df11636fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1fbb7d5a4a957b130c0835b09a6dba5c527e3b61844769b78f5517f4e4914b39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386962} {:answer/id #uuid "399ebddc-b942-5c6b-8d8e-df85655665f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d5df9fe81ebd1e656eefc97380ae646a1a4f64de51e4745bc31d822e022d65d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386963} {:answer/id #uuid "9b82c2d7-12f5-53f9-94db-a216cf94b533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/128c02e4d847e918cce92b38f4a7f09ffab6a11bf6f25ee7fd3480d6a6c3cac6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386964} {:answer/id #uuid "f54c1d72-e512-5d2c-944e-9d0b9b4f9323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/81bdcfd873677151067b3e2d542315d2f10f9b65f19a05ad3759dc5fe69a3704.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399ebddc-b942-5c6b-8d8e-df85655665f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d5df9fe81ebd1e656eefc97380ae646a1a4f64de51e4745bc31d822e022d65d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386963} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb0f45e-2d6d-59d5-95a0-428528782598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ca31a32-1c1a-5bad-882d-872d4b1f5188" :question/math-academy-id "q-119372" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{xy}?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7d/7d5df9fe81ebd1e656eefc97380ae646a1a4f64de51e4745bc31d822e022d65d.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/d0/d05d98b24783511dc6ff7518141e49d9cfefe96565f16b9d11b30d0e6fc0a5a4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84adfcaf-a598-5571-aa06-f6af588ed60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe57c0cc2d75729921eb46408e0e4a6932eba06c00034f2ec4c579c8c5627376.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386968} {:answer/id #uuid "d119e212-ad62-51f4-aa40-7ccda4c4d389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c959785ef067b41eba5e5ca5f0759acdd81c9f613c92854ba5937be6bced2c86.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386969} {:answer/id #uuid "e17b3836-865c-519c-88f1-9b2ec1f1d16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac12c64fe5282c78ea926eec823cec6b66c8fad9f153c4191d39ba7751e90e25.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386970} {:answer/id #uuid "373395c0-c96b-52a6-9531-5a58b40d8884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9cb42cde56fcbea0c489742cab1448890728dab584395129b21794115b1e1298.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386971} {:answer/id #uuid "48347676-3575-5e72-90f7-c8cf10b0c6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96817ea42e1d860db97d3ee6bca79e9a8b03a0fd413c87ebc5679f746da3166b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373395c0-c96b-52a6-9531-5a58b40d8884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9cb42cde56fcbea0c489742cab1448890728dab584395129b21794115b1e1298.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386971} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc934043-e8c4-5447-b0e7-d94aa4cef2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d1772040-8608-5c67-8243-2a903b40e108" :question/math-academy-id "q-119383" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $x$ given a fixed value of $y.$\n\n![](images/9c/9cb42cde56fcbea0c489742cab1448890728dab584395129b21794115b1e1298.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/9d/9d256bd6c44a9abc8489c360722b5c5e8243f0d4a9aa6fee03cad52e5334b385.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee9ad499-5aa4-5a3a-acbc-e49ac21406a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391528} {:answer/id #uuid "b2955172-b81c-5011-9701-e9bf938ea274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{s/m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391529} {:answer/id #uuid "decbd6c4-0fbe-5f80-9104-406758eb230d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391530} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5581a2-3f9c-5148-8b70-4faaa09845fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391531} {:answer/id #uuid "9195c8be-ff5f-5d44-bb08-28247be9de64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9ad499-5aa4-5a3a-acbc-e49ac21406a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391528} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4490809-5163-5471-a1d4-a55afc28a312" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391527} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50b58fcb-84d7-579f-880d-eb2423553ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391534} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5d9fd5-a8df-5113-baf9-e036a0442b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391535} {:answer/id #uuid "654583d2-f325-5d3b-9bf1-95a849ca6cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{4}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391536} {:answer/id #uuid "a003cd6c-ace7-5cea-850c-762788d62687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a003cd6c-ace7-5cea-850c-762788d62687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391537} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cec6877-9346-5efe-a85d-d63bfc7cf07e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14031906-5df2-543f-a91e-f54dba551c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391539} {:answer/id #uuid "69cee0c3-8008-5a0d-a9f2-37f86a67608c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391540} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc61919-e975-55cf-994c-bc8361ad199d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391541} {:answer/id #uuid "e24e6796-8ece-597a-9157-25358a0219a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391542} {:answer/id #uuid "0184e718-3d0a-5f0f-b11d-20a62daa4641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0184e718-3d0a-5f0f-b11d-20a62daa4641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391543} :answer-field/id #uuid "1502aa04-c760-583b-813d-02d5faca3f23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64f5cd17-fe8c-5f61-947e-78b369ea5b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-335179" :question/problem "A research container of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the container's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the container is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the container’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the container are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the body, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =20g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the body (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=20g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the body, we have\n\n$$\n20g-kv=20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nWriting in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =20g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{20}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nSo, substituting $v=8,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=8.2$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.2+\\frac{k}{20}\\cdot 8 & =9.8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{5} & =1.6 \\\\ k & =4\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e351540a-03e5-5560-8edb-4489b8437b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/327d6ae536cd6d76eff01387b51a4d2cc0522559d6ec6d02e3a86c2a3977f7b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394011} {:answer/id #uuid "617858dc-e3d0-5878-95c3-a12969db50fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b8e42e667d2fe4c713e92f879c9894e826fdbe3e6928addb3c4b36ce71991e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394012} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d0c0bb-d5d0-5d94-ac93-0530d2e8ddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7e267508d68c1d4eeb31509a4a2957e120cecab920481ba6dc2b0fa1f84433b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394013} {:answer/id #uuid "4d512f05-dd97-5052-a1ea-cf88826055eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3ddcc5784dc1fd60ba3c95ecda373678d00fc6c45e6b5e5655febcc130c37d95.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394014} {:answer/id #uuid "8caa0ceb-c764-5a3c-ad71-21838d736049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/2527ad3b4f6d0a1d84a9e907b5e3e9bddee439fbd17c2a1a4c3ca8c418f65293.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d512f05-dd97-5052-a1ea-cf88826055eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3ddcc5784dc1fd60ba3c95ecda373678d00fc6c45e6b5e5655febcc130c37d95.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394014} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a48c867-00e1-516c-a92b-1a0172abc1ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be176ed2-61aa-5d12-9259-7d0a7e6c43ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-80497" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x-2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-1),(2,-1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-1)}=(-2)-2(-1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=(2)-2(-1)=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=(2)-2(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6a/6a2d6f3812df2844171dacf1675d8e3d90167010cb2ffcf939f88d9cfcafe8a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eec0e316-618a-5c06-8ef7-151ecbbb1a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bfd9789a91f680d5105d9081e51103ffba5e986fa4a96331cde06df6d0c42854.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395116} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a52671-312b-5bd4-be3f-4aa0a30f8a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/543365359599203eb6f9e8824f6e3b2987a1fec9121568dd7a9098bfc16870f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395117} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1ba541-6bb3-5c12-a669-2035625bf666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3edf063230f100455638fa25f28a4c5e59e2e54884d9e273724b76ae483aa0bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395118} {:answer/id #uuid "956373bb-29e5-548d-9797-5b1c1b94804e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/631f9d7772354c6962f98e857371eac65d0fe36116bbae60dacd0db7ba01bc04.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395119} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6386d1-d302-5559-a668-c92b3aa1ae83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/7670c9662fec950b45f2f939ed416590d278875b168caea9d7e5b70f7c8c6a9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1a52671-312b-5bd4-be3f-4aa0a30f8a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/543365359599203eb6f9e8824f6e3b2987a1fec9121568dd7a9098bfc16870f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395117} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d543f0-8732-53ef-a971-f59ec7730788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53548ea4-2b92-5da8-9bc4-e7dc116ebef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96089" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=-2+2(1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=1+2(1)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2+2(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cc/cc55a08f71cd75f0dda356149a61e9ab78536ecbb4644b88f840122863b92b88.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1e8df4-9ee7-5f3f-87ce-6b27241b1f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/58a69833c7b4f627c0cfd436f5ff624ea15dfc0f68d548e94b987794f06a58a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395467} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6eb4ee-97c1-582b-8da7-2c61313e86ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/4191f22560c393dacad29a06e34b7c725800e27554e2c4ad7a9e40d9e56741e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395468} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2fa908-d317-59a8-b3b8-2b0d51743ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e45b8a64f56d831392ddc1eca4ddec922949d58e1c3f1f69c4db6238fe9a3200.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395469} {:answer/id #uuid "f663747e-7bbd-55ad-b290-2459b37f9e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/ab73df79e9518f68e27860a1655867d092255e604666c4153f3d950b30410fc5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395470} {:answer/id #uuid "06ef197b-ec6b-5859-aeed-bb60ff22e391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06ef197b-ec6b-5859-aeed-bb60ff22e391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395471} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5fb134a-fbef-51db-87b6-a286e83e96be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e290eb2-4c30-594d-9303-2ef73ece0ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98231" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $x$ given a fixed value of $y.$\n\n![](images/43/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/71/718f65dc7f4ccab0931d04855c647bd39beffc014f1f389896edf83352fcc988.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cb7b3b-ca02-57e0-a31c-2311ebcd6860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "ÍII only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396573} {:answer/id #uuid "21a7e756-add5-5d08-a0b6-865f25787d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396574} {:answer/id #uuid "0417384c-c1ac-529e-bf13-223231a54322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396575} {:answer/id #uuid "4737d57e-814c-5ac7-acfb-48ce13b5a3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396576} {:answer/id #uuid "95ce12e4-12c0-5c3c-9bc0-e72604c4d3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65cb7b3b-ca02-57e0-a31c-2311ebcd6860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "ÍII only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396573} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ee641f-c56c-5e39-8179-c1c054897293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8e4d2f85-2640-5963-a9fc-d023ea35768b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103178" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$\nII. $xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$\nIII. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065920c3-3946-51c8-a133-68edfc9cd6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396879} {:answer/id #uuid "0d22f601-64a5-507d-b229-bb37752252fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{s/m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396880} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a98c26-74a6-5cc7-bd61-0b52f4f896ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396881} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4de62e-7406-574c-9890-752f72463e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396882} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ca2c26-8790-5084-bf59-05857f6422a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "065920c3-3946-51c8-a133-68edfc9cd6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396879} :answer-field/id #uuid "af554e6b-2c53-5353-a213-81ba8542e5c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d84a91-a48c-5442-ba12-46ed4951dd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396885} {:answer/id #uuid "de3ab691-ae02-5e93-b61d-565249937a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396886} {:answer/id #uuid "fff9606c-86b6-58cf-8faa-74c6c2b26b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396887} {:answer/id #uuid "4e61cb73-419a-52d3-a9f7-333b057968e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396888} {:answer/id #uuid "876d4332-2399-5b6f-8c02-a57015c7a565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d84a91-a48c-5442-ba12-46ed4951dd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396885} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f9cc258-a83b-5feb-ad2a-e5812b2c952b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8cb5870-7d7e-5f93-9c6a-f0f887130056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{3}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396891} {:answer/id #uuid "35beaf44-402a-5d52-b1c7-4ebecfa4c887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{16}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396892} {:answer/id #uuid "51560195-657c-5865-98a3-9f67f3c4003a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396893} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e0ec5c-2874-5410-b0de-b4a739e627e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{4}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8cb5870-7d7e-5f93-9c6a-f0f887130056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{3}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396891} :answer-field/id #uuid "af529f9f-fe46-5674-b9c2-cb11914422f2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0989f91d-6c3f-5d3f-bed3-87412bdd7915" :question/math-academy-id "q-335748" :question/problem "A sensor pod of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the pod's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the pod’s velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $7.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the pod are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the body, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =16g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the body (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=16g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the body, we have\n\n$$\n16g-kv=16\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nWriting in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =16g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{16}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $7.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nSo, substituting $v=6,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=7.8$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8+\\frac{k}{16}\\cdot 6 & =9.8 \\\\ 7.8+\\frac{3k}{8} & =9.8 \\\\ \\frac{3k}{8} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{3}v=g.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15f378a-48fc-52fe-8823-93e1ecd6bfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473530} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0b8c78-df0e-5376-a1d5-062470f9b700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e15f378a-48fc-52fe-8823-93e1ecd6bfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8491ecc-d42f-5097-b247-4917384941a1" :answer-field/key "$10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11eae91c-5d78-5a31-ba0c-4a2e1724c2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473532} {:answer/id #uuid "5427f4e5-abc9-5196-b937-ea2a61a4979f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11eae91c-5d78-5a31-ba0c-4a2e1724c2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473532} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a6a6b8-f0c5-5e99-bb31-95a22ffc645a" :answer-field/key "$2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474161} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a748e074-f924-5d1c-9ca0-5cbd913edfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473534} {:answer/id #uuid "e22fc836-77eb-51c5-b108-fdca97c84f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22fc836-77eb-51c5-b108-fdca97c84f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473535} :answer-field/id #uuid "d122ca1e-6dcc-5fcd-92e2-84487547bfc4" :answer-field/key "$2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8fa44e0d-f47d-55dc-9787-703e1dcaa8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-90493" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194293e4-0f48-56b7-8094-db759002b936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "638f4f88-2af6-5eb2-9ed5-e5ceca460c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f8034-9dc3-50c6-bd5e-b2ea032262ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} {:answer/id #uuid "ada75cf0-6667-5096-ac74-83cbe5d47353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f9639-4822-59a3-b1ce-00790077ea5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129340" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c4d527-5e01-5264-8001-c16ba5c829f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453880} {:answer/id #uuid "8a788be2-6daa-5a9a-b61f-b090c8b8cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a788be2-6daa-5a9a-b61f-b090c8b8cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d46ebdc-4ea8-5b5d-a791-12ebf82b2b2b" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7662fdd-d050-5004-a857-2d86c30b4a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} {:answer/id #uuid "9233290a-d139-5d2d-91b5-3115178cf2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7662fdd-d050-5004-a857-2d86c30b4a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9a1015-1583-5e39-b729-2685f362e667" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f5a5f0-e3d5-58cb-a9a2-e0e244128177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} {:answer/id #uuid "6013b3d6-d8f8-5f12-8351-83ccb30ce208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f5a5f0-e3d5-58cb-a9a2-e0e244128177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} :answer-field/id #uuid "81040326-5134-5a42-8539-aac88ecd9a5e" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1408b7a6-0c53-5b8e-9133-67f17a92be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} {:answer/id #uuid "195220e5-a0bf-53a4-9715-d520b9cc06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} {:answer/id #uuid "4272af7f-33bd-5340-9cab-dc535b5219ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9919cf-e851-589f-8631-eb084d75d2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407832} {:answer/id #uuid "af13b1b6-a010-5d4f-9f5a-9200139b2880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4272af7f-33bd-5340-9cab-dc535b5219ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} :answer-field/id #uuid "356ed38c-7b44-5965-81ac-2c88dd16e0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-74955" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f90210-da8d-511b-9d38-b3d158c315d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "88b04b8f-a6d9-5b86-ba06-a0eb6a004245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa7bf-e6dc-52f1-8358-bfb499369454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e2afc-964e-5d9b-b663-5d2974b50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129343" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436086} {:answer/id #uuid "4b176e8d-dda0-56b4-82b1-b56ebe13e094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436089} {:answer/id #uuid "c65ea214-6d95-58fa-a971-d2654e7a972b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436107} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fb3b67-eae3-54a3-a2f2-8864cb03f007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436111} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc96a06-c359-5bf6-a8b3-a2cef416b538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436086} :answer-field/id #uuid "050f7890-b08d-5d96-a6f3-aa1024a4d86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15e24e7e-a45b-5130-9771-640280afca4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74953" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436076} {:answer/id #uuid "15697aa4-8790-5d84-b361-1fcd5c853cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436079} {:answer/id #uuid "3a28c3c9-2e36-51fc-96e3-067a7a2ef398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436083} {:answer/id #uuid "40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436085} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3eb38d-a93e-535b-a33a-1db8c0313fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436085} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b9cba1-39d8-5078-b34a-2ad5eaf58ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-74962" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416f53e7-0424-5030-ac7c-3c49b5372569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436087} {:answer/id #uuid "5aceb8dd-e06d-5296-8078-5ae4cc750061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860